WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.560
Session management protects authenticated user state after login.

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:08.740
Authentication establishes identity, but sessions carry that identity through later requests.

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:20.960
Because session identifiers and tokens can grant access, they must be generated securely, transmitted safely, stored carefully, validated server-side, and removed or renewed when security conditions change.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:25.900
Session management is the bridge between sign-in and every protected action that follows.

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:36.600
Strong session management combines secure identifiers, protected cookies, lifecycle management, monitoring, server-side validation, and careful framework configuration.

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:39.140
Use mature framework features where possible.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:46.000
Configure Secure, HttpOnly, SameSite, domain, path, and lifetime settings intentionally.

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:49.500
Apply idle and absolute timeouts according to risk.

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:53.000
Rotate or renew session state at sensitive transitions.

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:59.300
Invalidate sessions when logout, account, credential, or privilege events require it.

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:06.640
The goal is to preserve user identity securely throughout an authenticated session while minimizing exposure if a session is compromised.

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:16.220
That means balancing usability and security, treating tokens like credentials, avoiding unnecessary custom implementations, and testing the behaviors users and defenders rely on.

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:26.760
When teams review session lifecycle, cookie and token handling, logging, and authorization together, session security becomes a practical engineering discipline instead of an invisible assumption.
